# lemonardo-inkwell - Lemonardo Inkwell, dark mode -- the night shift at the light table
#
# GENERATED BY tools/make-lemonardo-ansi.py. Change the rules there,
# not the values here: tests/test_lemonardo_ansi.py re-derives this
# file from themes/lemonardo.json and fails on any difference, so a
# hand edit will be caught and then lost.
#
# EVERY HUE BELOW COMES FROM A MATERIAL AND FROM NOTHING ELSE. Each
# family's hue angle is read off a published reference value for a real
# pigment or drafting material; the generator then throws that value's
# lightness and chroma away and rebuilds both by rule. So what the
# pigment contributes is its hue identity, not a web swatch's opinion
# about how bright it should be. The reference hexes are NOT
# spectrophotometer readings of paint and must not be quoted as if
# they were; two are Wikipedia infobox values credited to Maerz and
# Paul, two are Wikipedia values with thinner provenance, and one
# (mauveine) is from a colour-name site and is the weakest of the set.
#
#   1/9  vermilion / cinnabar (HgS) -- the red of the construction-line pencil every animator roughed in
#        ref #ff4000, hue 34.5 [strong: Wikipedia infobox, credited to Maerz and Paul]
#   2/10  Paris green / emerald green (copper acetoarsenite) -- the brilliant cel-paint green
#        ref #50c878, hue 151.5 [strong: Wikipedia infobox, credited to Maerz and Paul]
#   3/11  chrome yellow (lead chromate, from crocoite) -- the paint-pot yellow of the period
#        ref #ffa700, hue 71.7 [medium: Wikipedia infobox]
#   4/12  cobalt blue (PB28, cobalt aluminate) -- the committed line, inked over the rough
#        ref #0047ab, hue 259.7 [medium: Wikipedia infobox]
#   5/13  mauveine / Perkin's aniline purple, 1856 -- the first synthetic dye, and the aniline family the studio's coloured inks came from
#        ref #8d029b, hue 323.4 [weak: color-name.com -- the WEAKEST source in this table]
#   6/14  verdigris (copper acetate) -- the blue-green of aged copper, and of the inks ground from it
#        ref #43b3ae, hue 191.0 [weak: Wikipedia infobox; that page carries a maintenance notice that it may conflate verdigris with patina]
#   sel  non-photo blue -- the drafting blue graphic-arts camera film cannot see, so a rough drawn in it needed no erasing before the clean-up line went down on the same sheet
#        ref #a4dded, hue 217.0 [strong: Wikipedia infobox]
#        Tried as slot 12, the blue family's bright tier -- the
#        committed cobalt line over the rough that does not print.
#        REJECTED: at hue 217.0 it sits between verdigris (191.0) and
#        Prussian blue (245.9), and slots 12 and 14 came out dE2000 8.5
#        apart, the closest pair in the palette by a factor of two. A
#        selection is a mark that is not part of the document and must
#        not print, which is what the pigment was for anyway.
#
# All six base accents sit at ONE Oklab lightness, 0.700, carrying
# 97% of the sRGB chroma available at that lightness for their own hue,
# capped at 0.18. A fraction and not a fixed number, because the sRGB
# ceiling at L 0.70 runs from 0.086 for cyan to 0.236 for magenta, and one
# absolute target would clamp cyan flat while magenta kept its full
# load; and then a cap as well, because the same fraction of a much
# larger ceiling gives one slot that reads as a light source next to
# neighbours that read as paint.
# The bright tier is the same hues at L 0.820 (+0.120).
#
# Gates this file passed at generation time. Re-run the test rather
# than believing this comment, which is a record and not a check:
#   base accents >= 4.5:1 vs ground, bright >= 3.5:1, slot 8 >= 3.2:1
#   foreground >= 6.0:1 vs ground and >= 4.5:1 over the selection
#   no two of the 16 slots closer than dE2000 9.0 AND Oklab 6.0
#   every accent >= dE2000 8.0 / Oklab 6.0 from the same
#   midori-night slot, and from the nearest value anywhere in that
#   theme. Both metrics, because each is blind where the other sees:
#   CIEDE2000 discounts pure saturation differences, which is the axis
#   these two languages differ on, and Oklab distance understates two
#   near-whites of different warmth, which is the neutrals.

